llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: a weak-linkage structor is emitted as its own function (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for the complete constructor and
destructor (C1, D1) of a class whose structor has weak linkage for the
linker: an inline definition, an implicit or an explicit template
instantiation. Upstream 4.0.0 replaces such a D1 by the D2 (RAUW) or
emits both in a C5/D5 COMDAT group, so the executable holds one body;
the PS4 compiler that built the game region kept clang 3.3's rule
(CGCXX.cpp, TryEmitDefinitionAsAlias: no alias to a linker-weak
target, no replacement) on its later code generator: D1 is emitted as
its own function that calls D2, which the optimizer inlines when D2 is
small and leaves as a tail jump when it is not, and the deleting
destructor D0 calls D2. A strong definition still becomes an alias.
Measured over every vtable of the game region (docs/DECISIONS.md D45):
507 strong aliases, 248 five-byte D1 thunks jumping to a D2 of 147 to
2,925 bytes, 905 D1 bodies of their own; nine test cases compiled with
3.3, 3.4.2, 3.5.2, 3.6.2, 3.7.1, 3.8.1, 3.9.1, 4.0.0 and 4.0.0 without
constructor aliases, and with this patch, which alone gives 3.3's
shapes in every case while the whole gate stays equal.

--- a/tools/clang/lib/CodeGen/ItaniumCXXABI.cpp
+++ b/tools/clang/lib/CodeGen/ItaniumCXXABI.cpp
@@ -3526,6 +3526,12 @@
   }
   llvm::GlobalValue::LinkageTypes Linkage = CGM.getFunctionLinkage(AliasDecl);
 
+  // PS4: a structor with weak linkage (inline, template instantiation) is
+  // emitted as its own function that calls the base variant, as clang 3.3
+  // emitted it; only a strong definition becomes an alias.
+  if (llvm::GlobalValue::isWeakForLinker(Linkage))
+    return StructorCodegen::Emit;
+
   if (llvm::GlobalValue::isDiscardableIfUnused(Linkage))
     return StructorCodegen::RAUW;
 
